onment": 8.3, "incumbency": -2.0, "money": -1.66}, "rating_margin": -15.3, "sigma_race": 6.5, "sigma_total": 7.15, "baseline_margin": -10.93, "analyst_adjustment": -2.07, "key_factors": ["R+9 district with a first-term incumbent and unanimous Safe/Solid R ratings from all ten expert raters", "Overwhelming fundraising gap: Harrigan raised $1.08M with $451K on hand versus Bell's $84K and $26K", "Lone public poll (Republican-sponsored, March) showed Harrigan up about 14 points", "Prediction markets price Bell's win probability near 6%"], "rationale": "The district's R+9 lean, a first-term incumbent who has outraised his challenger more than twelve to one, and ten of ten expert ratings at Safe or Solid Republican all point the same direction, and the only poll, though dated and partisan-sponsored, showed a double-digit Harrigan lead. The modest shift toward Safe R reflects that consensus plus a quiet October news flow featuring only routine incumbent activity.", "watch": "A late-breaking scandal, a sharply stronger Democratic national wave than current signs indicate, or major outside spending arriving in the district would be the clearest signal the race is tightening.", "confidence": "medium", "notes": ["No usable general-election polls; forecast rests on fundamentals and expert ratings.", "Incumbent Pat Harrigan (R) is running."]}, "overview": "North Carolina's 10th District leans firmly Republican, and first-term Representative Pat Harrigan enters the general election as a clear favorite. Every expert rating on record, from the Cook Political Report to the Economist, places the seat at Safe or Solid Republican, and prediction markets price Ashley Bell's chances in the single digits. Harrigan has raised more than one million dollars and holds over 450,000 dollars on hand, while Bell has raised about 84,000 dollars with negligible outside support.\n\nBell won a crowded Democratic primary in March and has drawn local media coverage, but no independent polling or significant outside spending has emerged to suggest competitiveness. The only public poll, conducted by a Republican-sponsored firm in March, showed Harrigan leading by double digits. A Libertarian candidate is also on the ballot. Absent an unexpected development, the district's fundamentals point to Republican retention.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.0414, "margin": -11.04}, {"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.0292, "margin": -13.0}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.0146, "margin": -14.0}, {"date": "2026-09-25", "p_dem": 0.0158, "margin": -14.0}, {"date": "2026-09-26", "p_dem": 0.0277, "margin": -13.0}, {"date": "2026-09-27", "p_dem": 0.0297, "margin": -13.0}, {"date": "2026-09-28", "p_dem": 0.0186, "margin": -13.0}, {"date": "2026-09-29", "p_dem": 0.0233, "margin": -13.0}, {"date": "2026-09-30", "p_dem": 0.0181, "margin": -13.0}, {"date": "2026-10-01", "p_dem": 0.0299, "margin": -13.0}, {"date": "2026-10-02", "p_dem": 0.0256, "margin": -13.0}, {"date": "2026-10-03", "p_dem": 0.0322, "margin": -12.0}, {"date": "2026-10-04", "p_dem": 0.0374, "margin": -13.0}]}